.Dd October 29, 2026
.Dt XFT_ATOMICS 3
.Os xft
.Sh NAME
.Nm xft_thread_fence ,
.Nm xft_signal_fence ,
.Nm xft_mutex_init ,
.Nm xft_mutex_lock ,
.Nm xft_mutex_unlock
.Nd mutexes and memory fences
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In atomics.h
.Ft void
.Fn xft_thread_fence "t_i32a memorder"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_signal_fence "t_i32a memorder"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_mutex_init "t_mutex *restrict mutex"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_mutex_lock "t_mutex *restrict mutex" "const t_mutex_type type"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_mutex_unlock "t_mutex *restrict mutex"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.In atomics.h
provides a single 32-bit mutex primitive and a pair of memory fences.
The mutex is built directly on C11 atomics and, on Linux, the
.Xr futex 2
system call; it does not depend on
.Xr pthread 3 .
.Pp
The types and state constants are declared in
.In types/atomic_types.h :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef volatile  int32_t __attribute__((__aligned__(64))) t_c_i32;
typedef volatile t_c_i32  t_mutex_nonsh;
typedef volatile t_i32a   t_mutex;

typedef enum e_mutex_type : t_u8
{
	xft_lock_fast,
	xft_lock_slow,
	xft_lock_busy,
}	t_mutex_type;

#define XFT_UNLOCKED  0
#define XFT_LOCKED    1
#define XFT_CONTESTED 2
.Ed
.Pp
.Vt t_mutex
is a 32-bit signed integer.
Its value is one of
.Dv XFT_UNLOCKED ,
.Dv XFT_LOCKED
or
.Dv XFT_CONTESTED :
.Dv XFT_UNLOCKED
means the mutex is free,
.Dv XFT_LOCKED
means it is held and no waiter has been recorded by the unlock path, and
.Dv XFT_CONTESTED
means it is held and at least one waiter is asleep on it through
.Xr futex 2 .
.Pp
.Dv XFT_MUTEX_SPIN
is the number of iterations
.Fn xft_mutex_lock
performs on the
.Dv xft_lock_fast
path before sleeping.
It defaults to 32 and can be redefined before
.In atomics.h
is included.
.Ss Mutex functions
.Fn xft_mutex_init
stores
.Dv XFT_UNLOCKED
into
.Fa mutex .
Nothing is allocated and no system call is made; a mutex in static storage
that has been zeroed is already in the same state.
.Pp
.Fn xft_mutex_lock
acquires
.Fa mutex ,
blocking until it is available.
Every call first attempts a single acquire compare-and-exchange from
.Dv XFT_UNLOCKED
to
.Dv XFT_LOCKED .
If it succeeds the function returns without touching the slow path.
If it fails, execution continues according to
.Fa type :
.Bl -tag -width "xft_lock_busy"
.It Dv xft_lock_fast
Spin up to
.Dv XFT_MUTEX_SPIN
times.
Each iteration performs a relaxed load; if the mutex reads as
.Dv XFT_UNLOCKED
the thread attempts a compare-and-exchange from
.Dv XFT_UNLOCKED
to
.Dv XFT_CONTESTED .
Between attempts the thread calls
.Fn xft_mutex_backoff
with the largest power of two not greater than the current iteration
count.
If the spin fails, the thread exchanges the mutex into
.Dv XFT_CONTESTED
and, on failure, sleeps in
.Fn xft_futex_wait
with the expected value
.Dv XFT_CONTESTED .
It retries the exchange after each wake-up.
This is the general purpose choice.
.It Dv xft_lock_busy
Spin forever, attempting the same
.Dv XFT_CONTESTED
exchange and backing off between attempts.
No system call is made.
Suitable when the critical section is short and contention is expected to
be rare.
.It Dv xft_lock_slow
Exchange the mutex into
.Dv XFT_CONTESTED
and, if the previous value was not
.Dv XFT_UNLOCKED ,
sleep in
.Fn xft_futex_wait .
There is no spin phase.
Suitable when the lock is expected to be heavily contended.
.El
.Pp
The
.Fa type
argument selects the failure path only; the initial compare-and-exchange
from
.Dv XFT_UNLOCKED
to
.Dv XFT_LOCKED
is shared by all three.
Passing a value that is not one of the three enumerators reaches
.Ql __builtin_unreachable .
.Pp
.Fn xft_mutex_unlock
releases
.Fa mutex .
It performs a release fetch-and-subtract of one.
If the previous value was
.Dv XFT_CONTESTED ,
the mutex is stored back to
.Dv XFT_UNLOCKED
and one thread is woken via
.Fn xft_futex_wake .
If the previous value was
.Dv XFT_LOCKED ,
the fetch-and-subtract leaves the mutex at
.Dv XFT_UNLOCKED
and no wake-up is issued.
.Ss Memory fences
.Fn xft_thread_fence
issues a hardware memory fence with the memory order given by
.Fa memorder .
.Fn xft_signal_fence
issues a compiler fence with the same argument.
.Ss Backoff
The internal
.Fn xft_mutex_backoff
executes
.Fa n
relaxation instructions:
.Ql pause
on x86_64,
.Ql yield
on aarch64, or an empty memory-clobbering
.Ql asm
statement elsewhere.
The spin loops call it with a count derived from the current iteration
number through
.Fn xft_last_pow2 ,
so the number of instructions in a single backoff step stays bounded by
the loop's own iteration cap.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return nothing.
.Fn xft_mutex_lock
blocks until it holds the mutex; it has no failure mode.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft.h>

static t_mutex	g_lock;

void	init(void)
{
	xft_mutex_init(&g_lock);
}

void	critical_section(void)
{
	xft_mutex_lock(&g_lock, xft_lock_fast);
	/* ... */
	xft_mutex_unlock(&g_lock);
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xft_os 3 ,
.Xr xft_threads 3 ,
.Xr xft 7
.Sh CAVEATS
.Fn xft_mutex_lock
with a
.Fa type
outside
.Dv xft_lock_fast ,
.Dv xft_lock_slow
and
.Dv xft_lock_busy
reaches
.Ql __builtin_unreachable
and compiles to undefined behaviour at the call site.
.Pp
.Fn xft_mutex_unlock
does not check that the mutex is held.
Calling it on an unlocked mutex performs a release fetch-and-subtract on
.Dv XFT_UNLOCKED ,
leaving the mutex underflowed.
.Pp
The mutex does not track its owner and is not recursive; locking it twice
from the same thread deadlocks.
.Pp
.Dv xft_lock_busy
mutexes can starve other threads on a uniprocessor or an over-subscribed
system, since the spin loop never yields through the kernel.
